35,2,1,talk,Midori,,#### Turn 1 of 4,"{""words"":5,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,#### Turn 1 of 4,"{""words"":5,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,May,,- **May**: The 2018 data lift was a disaster. Mismatched codes haunted us for a year.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Misaki,,#### Turn 1 of 4,"{""words"":5,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"- **Benjamin**: The invoices confirm my fear: bundled deals held steady, but separate add-ons climbed yearly.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Midori,,#### Turn 2 of 4,"{""words"":5,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,- **Ryuji**: The November through January freeze is absolute. Any cutover then means two days of paper dispatch.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,May,,"- **May**: @Ryuji, that freeze window is a hard constraint. We can’t schedule a migration during peak season.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"- **Misaki**: @Ryuji, that timing is critical. But my findings show two key admins retiring this year, leaving us dangerously thin.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Benjamin, that confirms it. We are bleeding money on add-ons while the core system rots. We need a fixed scope.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Midori,,"- **Midori**: @Benjamin, that pricing trap is real. But my support review shows we had no owner for two days last winter.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Midori, unowned support is a disaster waiting to happen. We need a single throat to choke when the yard freezes.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,May,,"- **May**: @Ryuji, agreed. But remember the warehouse rules changed twice last year. A rigid system will break under that pressure.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"- **Misaki**: @May, rigid systems fail, but so do people. My supervisor data shows they quietly dropped the last rollout’s steps within weeks.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Misaki, that’s the human cost I can’t ignore. If the team abandons the new process, we’re just paying for a more expensive failure.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Midori,,"- **Midori**: @Benjamin, exactly. We need a vendor who owns the whole lifecycle, not just the initial sale.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"@Midori, agreed. But ownership means nothing if the hardware can’t talk to the new software. My yard trial failed because the devices were too old.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,May,,"- **May**: @Ryuji, old hardware is a trap. If the new system needs modern devices, that’s a hidden capital cost we haven’t budgeted for.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Misaki,,"@May, hardware costs are real, but if the supervisors ignore the new steps, the devices are just expensive paperweights.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@Misaki, true, but if the hardware fails, the supervisors have nothing to ignore. We need a vendor who handles both the code and the metal.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
