59,3,,status,Misaki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Midori,,- **Midori**: Candidates are here. Meridian’s fixed configuration and regional team seem to address our support ownership gap directly.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"- **Ryuji**: Meridian’s fixed config sounds rigid. If our yard devices are already a mess, a locked-down system might just break faster.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,May,,"- **May**: @Ryuji, fixed config sounds safe, but remember our rules changed twice last year. Rigid means we’re stuck with the old way.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Misaki,,"- **Misaki**: @May, rigidity is dangerous, but so is a partner firm handling training. My supervisors already ignored the last rollout’s steps.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Midori, Meridian’s regional team is a start, but I need to see if their fixed configuration locks out the add-ons that quietly inflate our bills.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Midori,,"- **Midori**: @Benjamin, valid point. But Tessera’s partner firm handles migration and training, which might solve our data lift and staffing gaps.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Midori, a partner firm is a liability. If the data lift fails, who owns the yard? I need direct control, not a subcontractor.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,May,,"- **May**: @Ryuji, you’re right to be wary. But if the partner firm is the one who botched the 2018 lift, we’re just buying the same mistake again.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"- **Misaki**: @Ryuji, I agree. But if we hire in-house, we’re stretching our remaining admins thinner. The partner firm is a risk, but so is our current staffing.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Ryuji, direct control is expensive. If we self-manage, we pay for the hardware, the staff, and the downtime. I need to see if that bill is sustainable.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Midori,,"- **Midori**: @Benjamin, sustainable is relative. Corvus’s modular approach lets us pay only for what we use, avoiding the bundled bloat.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Midori, modular means four separate contracts. Last winter, four suppliers meant two days of silence. I won’t gamble the yard on that fragmentation.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,May,,"@Ryuji, fragmentation is a nightmare, but Corvus lets us swap just the broken piece. We don’t have to rebuild the whole house to fix a leaky roof.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""name_label"",""name"":""May"",""self"":true,""raw"":""May: @Ryuji, fragmentation is a nightmare, but Corvus lets us swap just the broken piece. We don’t have to rebuild the whole house to fix a leaky roof.""}]}"
73,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"@May, swapping pieces sounds efficient, but if the modules don’t talk, we’re back to unowned outages. I need a single point of accountability.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Misaki, accountability is a luxury. Modular licensing is a trap; those separate pieces climbed yearly in our invoices. I won’t pay for fragmentation.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Midori,,"- **Midori**: @Benjamin, you’re right about the cost. But Anchor’s on-site engineering team might finally give us the single owner we’ve been missing.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"@Midori, on-site engineering is the only option that respects our yard reality. If they are here, they fix the hardware and the code together.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,May,,"- **May**: @Ryuji, on-site is good, but if they deploy on our own servers, we own the upgrade risk. Remember the dispatch tool? We skipped three windows.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Misaki,,"@May, that upgrade risk is real, but with two admins retiring, we can’t afford to be the ones fixing it. Their presence is our safety net.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, you’re right about the upgrade risk. But on our servers, we control the scope. No surprise add-ons, no subscription creep. Just a fixed, auditable bill.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
